The composite of the embedding i_∞ with the projection j_∞ is the identity Scott map on the function space [D_∞ → D_∞].

Extensionality of Scott maps, used twice, reduces the identity to the equality of i_∞(j_∞(f)) at a point z with f(z). Write r_n for the composite of the level-n inverse-limit embedding with the level-n projection. The sequence r_n is monotone because each level embedding lies below the next, and every element of the inverse limit equals the supremum of its images under the r_n. A supremum of Scott maps evaluates pointwise, and a Scott map passes inside a monotone supremum indexed by the natural numbers because the range of a monotone sequence is directed. The value j_∞(f) is the supremum of the embeddings of the conjugates j_{∞k} ∘ f ∘ i_{k∞}, and the coordinate lemma for the inverse limit, applied to the successor compatibility of those conjugates, identifies the (n+1)-st component of j_∞(f) with the n-th conjugate. Each summand of i_∞ at j_∞(f), evaluated at z, is therefore r_n(f(r_n(z))). The families m ↦ f(r_m(z)), m ↦ r_n(f(r_m(z))), and n ↦ r_n(f(r_m(z))) are monotone. Expanding f(z) as the supremum of r_k(f(z)), replacing z by the supremum of r_m(z), and passing f and then each r_k inside those suprema produces a monotone double supremum, which equals its diagonal. That diagonal is i_∞(j_∞(f)) at z.
